The leftmost lamp is blue with probability \(3/6\). Given that, 3 of the 5 remaining lamps are red, so the probability of blue on the left and red on the right is \((3/6)(3/5)=3/10\).
The choice of which lamps are on is independent of color. The leftmost lamp is off with probability \(3/6\); given that, 3 of the remaining 5 lamps are on, so the rightmost is on with probability \(3/5\). Multiplying the color and switch probabilities gives \((3/10)(3/6)(3/5)=9/100\).
Final answer: \dfrac{9}{100}
